* .NOISE smoke test: resistive divider, the case with an exact closed form.
*
*   V1 in 0 DC 1        (ideal source: in noise analysis it is a SHORT)
*   R1 in out 10k
*   R2 out 0 10k
*
* Noise sources: both resistors, 4kT/R (white).
* An ideal voltage source is a short for noise, so node `in` sits at 0 V and
* every generator sees the PARALLEL pair R1||R2 = 5k as its transfer
* resistance (R1's noise current returns through R1 or through the shorted
* source; a current injected at `out` returns through R1 or R2).
*
*   S_out(f) = (R1||R2)^2 * 4kT * (1/R1 + 1/R2) = 8.2875e-17 V^2/Hz
*   gain     = V(out)/V(in) = R2/(R1+R2) = 1/2
*   S_in     = S_out/gain^2 = 4kT(R1+R2) = 3.3151e-16 V^2/Hz
*              (the textbook equivalent input noise of a resistive divider)
*
* At 300.15 K and R = 10k: 4kT/R = 1.65755e-24 A^2/Hz
*   S_out = 8.2875e-17 V^2/Hz  ( 9.1039 nV/sqrt(Hz))
*   S_in  = 3.3151e-16 V^2/Hz  (18.2077 nV/sqrt(Hz))
*
* The .NOISE line carries pts_per_summary=1, so the CSV also has one column
* per noise generator.
V1 in 0 DC 1
R1 in out 10k
R2 out 0 10k
.NOISE V(out) V1 DEC 10 1 1e6 1
.END